This looks already fixed on `main`, by 8fc86e0 ("fix: sanitize proxy failures and default CCTV launcher to loopback", PR #289), which merged 2026-09-11 — a few hours after this was filed.

`error.upstreamBody` no longer exists anywhere in `vite.config.js`. The failure path this issue quotes now sends a fixed generic body (`vite.config.js:1752-1766`):

```js
} catch (error) {
  // Log only a bounded status, never upstream bodies, URLs, or credentials.
  const status = Number.isInteger(error?.upstreamStatus) ? error.upstreamStatus : 502;
  if (!request.shared) console.warn(`[launch-library-proxy] refresh failed (HTTP ${status})${stale ? ' — serving stale cache' : ''}`);
  if (stale) {
    send(res, 200, stale.body, 'STALE-ERROR');
    return;
  }
  send(
    res,
    status,
    JSON.stringify({ error: 'Launch Library 2 unavailable' }),
    'NONE',
  );
}
```

So the acceptance criterion holds: on upstream failure with no stale cache, the client gets a generic message and the upstream status, never the raw body. The same commit also bounded the server-side log to just the status code, so the upstream body is no longer interpolated there either.

There's regression coverage for it too — `src/proxyErrorResponses.test.mjs` asserts across 401/429/500 in both development and preview that the response body is exactly `{ error: 'Launch Library 2 unavailable' }` and that neither the body nor the logs contain the upstream fixture text.

